# Database migrations (Railway Postgres)

## Files
- `backend/database` is the self-hosted Railway Postgres schema (GoTrue/PostgREST/storage-api); it is NOT Supabase.com.
- Make every schema or data change as a **new** file in `backend/database/migrations/`, named `YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_short_snake_description.sql`. Its timestamp must be later than the newest existing file.
- **Never edit, rename or delete an existing migration.** It may already be applied on Railway (history lives in `public.app_schema_migrations`). To fix a mistake, write another migration.
- `backend/scripts/apply-migrations.mjs` (`npm run db:migrate`) has a `SKIP_FILES` list of LIMS-only migrations that must not run on this database. Don't change it without asking.
- Don't add `supabase/config.toml` or use any Supabase CLI command (the project is Railway-only).

## SQL style (idempotent, safe to re-run)
- Use `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, `ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS` and `CREATE [UNIQUE] INDEX IF NOT EXISTS`.
- Run `DROP POLICY IF EXISTS ...` before each `CREATE POLICY ...`. Write seeds with `ON CONFLICT ... DO NOTHING/UPDATE`.
- Where an object may be missing in some environments, wrap the statement in `DO $$ ... EXCEPTION WHEN undefined_table THEN NULL; END $$;`.
- Start each file with a header comment explaining the why. Data-changing steps (dedupe, delete, backfill) must be re-runnable and called out to Amit before he applies them.
- After adding or renaming columns used by the app, end with `NOTIFY pgrst, 'reload schema';` so PostgREST picks them up.

## Every new table
- `id uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()`
- Audit fields: `created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()` and `updated_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()`. Keep `updated_at` current with a trigger that reuses an existing function (grep the migrations for `set_updated_at` / `touch_updated_at`) instead of creating a new one. For user-entered rows, add `created_by uuid REFERENCES auth.users(id) ON DELETE SET NULL` (and `updated_by` where edits matter).
- **RLS on every table**: `ALTER TABLE public.<t> ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;` plus explicit policies. Since S4 (`20261008180000_s4_masters_settings_rls.sql`), use separate SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE policies built on `app_module_level()` / `app_can_edit()` / `app_is_admin()` (settings = admin-only writes). Never add new `USING (true)` write policies; older `<t>_authenticated_all` policies are legacy. Only grant to `anon` for intentionally public data (as with the website CMS public read), and say so in the header comment.
- **Integrity**:
  - Every FK gets an index and a deliberate `ON DELETE`: CASCADE for child or detail rows, RESTRICT or SET NULL for masters that transactions reference.
  - Natural keys (client `company_name`, GST number, IS number + year, document numbers) get UNIQUE constraints or indexes, case-insensitive (`lower(trim(...))`) where duplicates differ only by case.
  - Enum-like text columns get `CHECK` constraints.
- Storage buckets: create them with `INSERT INTO storage.buckets ... ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING`, with `storage.objects` policies scoped by `bucket_id`. `20261004120000_bis_project_files.sql` is the reference.

## After a schema change
- Update the matching TypeScript types (`frontend/web/src/features/**/types.ts`), the `select(...)` column lists, and any CSV import/export headers.
- Don't apply to production yourself unless asked. Tell Amit how: `npm run db:migrate`, with `DATABASE_URL` set or the Railway CLI linked.
